1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,114
On a hot July day in 1518, a woman named

2
00:00:09,114 --> 00:00:13,472
Frau Troffea stepped out of her house in Strasbourg, and

3
00:00:13,472 --> 00:00:14,640
began to dance.

4
00:00:14,990 --> 00:00:16,510
There was no music.

5
00:00:16,510 --> 00:00:18,510
There was no celebration.

6
00:00:18,510 --> 00:00:20,990
Her husband begged her to stop.

7
00:00:20,990 --> 00:00:21,870
She didn't.

8
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:26,844
She danced until her feet were swollen and bloody.

9
00:00:26,844 --> 00:00:29,803
She collapsed, slept, woke up...

10
00:00:29,803 --> 00:00:32,300
and danced again. For days.

11
00:00:32,650 --> 00:00:36,098
And then something far more frightening happened.

12
00:00:36,098 --> 00:00:38,490
Her neighbors started dancing too.

13
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:42,324
Within a week, more than thirty people were twisting and

14
00:00:42,324 --> 00:00:44,067
leaping through the streets.

15
00:00:44,067 --> 00:00:47,800
By August, some chroniclers claimed there were four hundred.

16
00:00:48,150 --> 00:00:52,176
The city's response was so strange it almost sounds like

17
00:00:52,176 --> 00:00:54,117
a joke. They built a stage.

18
00:00:54,117 --> 00:00:57,999
They hired musicians. They paid strong men to hold the

19
00:00:57,999 --> 00:00:58,790
dancers up.

20
00:00:59,140 --> 00:01:03,726
Today, you'll find the same chilling claim almost everywhere this

21
00:01:03,726 --> 00:01:07,324
story is told: that at its peak, the dancing plague

22
00:01:07,324 --> 00:01:09,300
killed fifteen people a day.

23
00:01:09,650 --> 00:01:12,777
But when you go back to the records written in

24
00:01:12,777 --> 00:01:16,108
Strasbourg that summer, something doesn't add up.

25
00:01:16,108 --> 00:01:18,283
So what really happened in 1518?

26
00:01:18,283 --> 00:01:21,886
And why did hundreds of people dance until they could

27
00:01:21,886 --> 00:01:22,770
barely stand?

28
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:26,729
To understand the dancers, you have to understand the city

29
00:01:26,729 --> 00:01:27,600
they lived in.

30
00:01:27,950 --> 00:01:31,728
Strasbourg in 1518 was a free imperial city on the

31
00:01:31,728 --> 00:01:36,110
Rhine, crowned by one of the tallest cathedrals in Europe.

32
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:38,442
But its people were suffering.

33
00:01:38,442 --> 00:01:40,424
Harvests had failed for years.

34
00:01:40,424 --> 00:01:44,718
Bread prices soared, and families sold everything they owned just

35
00:01:44,718 --> 00:01:45,180
to eat.

36
00:01:45,530 --> 00:01:49,263
Disease moved through the region in waves.

37
00:01:49,263 --> 00:01:53,530
Smallpox. Leprosy. And a terrifying new illness:

38
00:01:53,530 --> 00:01:54,330
syphilis.

39
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:58,364
To the people of the Rhineland, none of this was

40
00:01:58,364 --> 00:02:01,050
random. It was punishment from God.

41
00:02:01,050 --> 00:02:04,120
And one saint was feared above the rest.

42
00:02:04,470 --> 00:02:08,078
Saint Vitus. It was believed he could curse a sinner

43
00:02:08,078 --> 00:02:11,270
with a compulsion to dance that would not end.

44
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:15,065
As the dancing spread, the city council turned to its

45
00:02:15,065 --> 00:02:15,780
physicians.

46
00:02:16,130 --> 00:02:20,092
Their verdict was not witchcraft, and not the stars.

47
00:02:20,092 --> 00:02:24,130
It was a natural disease, caused by overheated blood.

48
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:26,529
And the cure for overheated blood?

49
00:02:26,529 --> 00:02:28,880
The dancers would have to dance it out.

50
00:02:29,230 --> 00:02:32,568
So the city cleared two guild halls, built a wooden

51
00:02:32,568 --> 00:02:36,234
stage near the horse market, and hired musicians to play

52
00:02:36,234 --> 00:02:37,150
day and night.

53
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:38,691
It did not work.

54
00:02:38,691 --> 00:02:40,328
The music drew crowds.

55
00:02:40,328 --> 00:02:43,900
And some in those crowds began to dance as well.

56
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:50,218
By early August, the council reversed course.

57
00:02:50,218 --> 00:02:53,070
Public music and dancing were banned.

58
00:02:53,420 --> 00:02:56,768
The dancers were taken by wagon to a shrine of

59
00:02:56,768 --> 00:03:00,989
Saint Vitus in the hills near Saverne, given small crosses

60
00:03:00,989 --> 00:03:03,900
and red shoes, and led around the altar.

61
00:03:04,250 --> 00:03:08,010
And slowly, by early September, the dancing stopped.

62
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:10,880
So what about the famous claim?

63
00:03:10,880 --> 00:03:13,400
Fifteen dead, every single day?

64
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:18,676
The records written in Strasbourg that summer, the council's notes,

65
00:03:18,676 --> 00:03:23,750
the physicians' reports, the sermons, describe the dancing in detail.

66
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:27,468
What they do not clearly record is a body count.

67
00:03:27,468 --> 00:03:31,118
The figure of fifteen a day appears only in accounts

68
00:03:31,118 --> 00:03:32,100
written later.

69
00:03:32,450 --> 00:03:36,966
Exhaustion, heart failure, or infected wounds may well have killed

70
00:03:36,966 --> 00:03:38,334
some of the dancers.

71
00:03:38,334 --> 00:03:42,234
But that famous statistic rests on far weaker ground than

72
00:03:42,234 --> 00:03:42,850
it seems.

73
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:47,045
One popular theory blames ergot, a fungus that grows on

74
00:03:47,045 --> 00:03:50,400
damp rye, and contains chemicals related to LSD.

75
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:54,024
But historian John Waller points out a problem.

76
00:03:54,024 --> 00:03:57,089
Ergot poisoning cuts off blood to the limbs.

77
00:03:57,089 --> 00:04:00,990
Its victims could barely walk, let alone dance for days.

78
00:04:01,340 --> 00:04:03,208
His explanation is darker.

79
00:04:03,208 --> 00:04:07,302
A mass psychogenic illness, in a population pushed to its

80
00:04:07,302 --> 00:04:08,380
breaking point.

81
00:04:08,730 --> 00:04:13,064
People who believed, completely, that Saint Vitus could curse them

82
00:04:13,064 --> 00:04:16,742
into dancing slipped into a trance, and did exactly what

83
00:04:16,742 --> 00:04:17,530
they feared.

84
00:04:17,880 --> 00:04:21,192
The stage made it worse, because it put the dance

85
00:04:21,192 --> 00:04:25,113
on display. The shrine ended it, because it gave believers

86
00:04:25,113 --> 00:04:26,600
a ritual they trusted.

87
00:04:26,950 --> 00:04:30,607
The dancing plague was not a poison, or a monster.

88
00:04:30,607 --> 00:04:34,630
It was what happens when fear spreads faster than food.

89
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:38,387
Hundreds of ordinary people danced until they collapsed.

90
00:04:38,387 --> 00:04:41,855
Not because something was done to them, but because their

91
00:04:41,855 --> 00:04:43,620
minds had nowhere left to go.
